[{"id":77158,"sender_id":647,"sender_full_name":"Ali Caglayan","avatar_url":"https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/a6454cf49f9cd8b51a43e333a3bcad2b?d=identicon&version=1","content":"<p><a href=\"#narrow/channel/15-Mathematics.2C-Physics-.26-Philosophy/topic/Having.20an.20oo-left.20adjoint/near/77158\">#4</a> So I had a chat with Emily and it turns out there is a way to make this precise:</p>\n<p>A functor <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>:</mo><mi>C</mi><mo>→</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">U : C \\to D</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.109em;\">U</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">:</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0715em;\">C</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">→</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0278em;\">D</span></span></span></span> has a left adjoint if for all <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">X</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0785em;\">X</span></span></span></span> in <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">D</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0278em;\">D</span></span></span></span>, the comma category <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>U</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">X / U</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:1em;vertical-align:-0.25em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0785em;\">X</span><span class=\"mord\">/</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.109em;\">U</span></span></span></span> has an initial object <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">X</mi></mrow><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(\\mathrm{FX}, \\eta_X)</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:1em;vertical-align:-0.25em;\"></span><span class=\"mopen\">(</span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathrm\">FX</span></span><span class=\"mpunct\">,</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.1667em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0359em;\">η</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.3283em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.55em;margin-left:-0.0359em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal mtight\" style=\"margin-right:0.0785em;\">X</span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.15em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mclose\">)</span></span></span></span>.</p>\n<p>Then you should be able to stick an oo infront of everything.</p>\n<p>This is proposition 4.1.5 in <a href=\"http://www.math.jhu.edu/~eriehl/elements.pdf\">her new book</a>.</p>","timestamp":1558903081,"subject":"Having an oo-left adjoint","stream_id":15,"display_recipient":"Mathematics, Physics & Philosophy","reactions":[]},{"id":77159,"sender_id":647,"sender_full_name":"Ali Caglayan","avatar_url":"https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/a6454cf49f9cd8b51a43e333a3bcad2b?d=identicon&version=1","content":"<p><a href=\"#narrow/channel/15-Mathematics.2C-Physics-.26-Philosophy/topic/Having.20an.20oo-left.20adjoint/near/77159\">#5</a> Am I correct in thinking that because we are considering oo-initial objects, we typically don’t even need to consider higher coherences?</p>","timestamp":1558903335,"subject":"Having an oo-left adjoint","stream_id":15,"display_recipient":"Mathematics, Physics & Philosophy","reactions":[]},{"id":77179,"sender_id":10,"sender_full_name":"Mike Shulman","avatar_url":"https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/ede34282f405f902e12fa7e6cf336572?d=identicon&version=1","content":"<p><a href=\"#narrow/channel/15-Mathematics.2C-Physics-.26-Philosophy/topic/Having.20an.20oo-left.20adjoint/near/77179\">#6</a> That probably depends on your definition of <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\infty</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.4306em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">∞</span></span></span></span>-initial…</p>","timestamp":1559033057,"subject":"Having an oo-left adjoint","stream_id":15,"display_recipient":"Mathematics, Physics & Philosophy","reactions":[]}]